<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Merkit confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1218 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of forest-steppe tribes south of Lake Baikal, sworn enemies of Genghis Khan after they kidnapped his wife Börte.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Merkits lived along the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selenga-river">Selenga</a> river south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-baikal">Lake Baikal</a>, on the edge of the Siberian forest. They are known from the 12th century as three allied tribes, the Uduyit, Uwas and Qa'at, speaking a Mongolic language.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Merkit society combined herding with hunting and forest resources, under tribal chiefs with no permanent central government; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toqtoa-beki">Toqto'a Beki</a> of the Uduyit was their most powerful leader. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin's</a> father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesugei">Yesügei</a> had stolen his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoelun">Hö'elün</a> from a Merkit, and in revenge the Merkits abducted Temüjin's bride <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borte">Börte</a>, who was rescued with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/keraite-khanate">Keraite Khanate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamukha">Jamukha</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> defeated the Merkits repeatedly from 1204. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toqtoa-beki">Toqto'a Beki</a> was killed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> in 1208, and his sons fled west towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Qipchaks</a>. Mongol forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and Jochi destroyed the last Merkit groups in 1217 to 1218, absorbing the survivors into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1100 CE – 1218 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.59%;width:2.35%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1218 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Toqto'a Beki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mongolic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>, <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Merkits</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1159&amp;focus=merkit-confederation" aria-label="Merkit confederation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5637 229 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Merkit confederation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7559 780h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5957 485h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5957 485h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1159&amp;focus=merkit-confederation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

